\title{multiScaleAC: Multi-Scale Spatial Autocorrelation with Moran's I}
\description{
Computes bivariate Moran's I as a function of Gaussian kernel bandwidth,
producing a functional curve \eqn{I(h)} over spatial scales instead of a
single value at one arbitrarily chosen scale. This removes the need to pick a
radius, bandwidth, or number of nearest neighbours, a choice that otherwise
biases the result.
}
\details{
Weights come directly from the kernel, row standardised, with
\strong{no minimum weight threshold}, so no cells or spots are dropped from
the weight matrix. That is the main methodological difference from approaches
that filter low kernel density estimates before computing the statistic.
}
\section{Getting started}{

\code{\link{multiScaleAC}} is the main entry point; it returns a
\code{\linkS4class{kernelcomm_result}}. Add inference with
\code{\link{compute_significance}}, correct across tests with
\code{\link{adjust_pvalues}}, inspect with \code{plot} and \code{summary},
and hand off to functional data analysis with \code{\link{export_mxfda}}.
Use \code{\link{suggest_bandwidths}} rather than guessing bandwidths.
}

\section{Choosing bandwidths}{

Bandwidths are in the same units as \code{coords}, and getting that scale
wrong is the most common error with this method. The bundled
\code{\link{ccRCCExample}} data is in \emph{millimetres}, spanning about
1 mm, so useful bandwidths are roughly 0.01 to 0.25. Coordinates supplied in
micrometres need bandwidths a thousand times larger. If a bandwidth is far
too large, every pairwise weight approaches 1, the kernel becomes a constant
matrix, and \eqn{I(h)} collapses toward zero for every gene pair.
\code{\link{qc_neighborhoods}} reports the effective neighbourhood size, so
this is visible before it is mistaken for a biological result.
}

\section{Package name}{

The package is \code{multiScaleAC}, matching the companion manuscript. The
source repository remains named \code{KernelCommunication}, which is the URL
cited in the paper.
}

